WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.960
- Medical sketches are
really like medical memes,

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.250
just reenacted.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.083
And guess what?

00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:06.950
On today's video, I'm
gonna be reacting some

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.850
of the most popular medical sketches

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.430
on YouTube, excluding SNL

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.810
'cause we've done those
already, link down below.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.820
But first, I have a major
announcement for you.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.910
I am looking to hire another editor.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.320
We want you to join the team.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.600
All the qualifications
and information is listed

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:21.790
down below in the description.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.070
So if you think you're right for the job,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:23.970
shoot me an email.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:26.073
By the way, if you get this
video to 100,000 likes,

00:00:26.073 --> 00:00:28.700
I might make a medical sketch of my own.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.910
- Hello patient, I'm Dr. Edith Celburn.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.100
- Hi Dr. Celburn.
(audience laughs)

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.800
- You're about to undergo an operation

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.600
to remove your appendix.

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.930
Just like this patient here,

00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:41.550
you'll be under anesthesia
and completely unconscious.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.630
- He doesn't look unconscious.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.461
- Actually, I am still kind of conscious.

00:00:46.461 --> 00:00:47.294
(audience laughs)

00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:48.127
- I can fix that.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.560
There we are, sleepy time.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.820
- Why is the surgeon doing the anesthesia?

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:56.510
There should be an anesthesiologist

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:57.627
in the room monitoring the patient.

00:00:57.627 --> 00:00:58.730
Lik a big function

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.460
of having an anesthesiologist present

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:01.890
is the fact that it allows the surgeon

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:03.560
to focus on the operation

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.970
and the anesthesiologist on
the sedation of the patient,

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.840
making sure that you're
not giving too much

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.190
where you're slowing down the
heart rate or the breathing.

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.120
Here, it looks like the
doctor just does everything.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.054
- Now we make the incision right here.

00:01:14.054 --> 00:01:15.230
(scalpel cracking)

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.770
- Why are there cracking noises?

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.530
Why is the mask not up?

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:20.720
Why is that happening right now?

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.111
- Oops, got a little leakage there.

00:01:23.111 --> 00:01:23.944
(audience laughs)
- Leakage?

00:01:23.944 --> 00:01:25.850
- In this case I've
made the incision bigger

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.440
so we have plenty of elbow
room to scoop out all the parts

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.220
of your insides that have gone bad.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.053
Like this one.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.140
And this one.
(audience laughing)

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.530
- Filled salamis?

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.110
- Not even sure what this is.

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.730
Oh well, out it goes.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.340
Now we'll move some nasty
bones out of the way.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.320
- What bones are in the
abdomen that you're taking out

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.410
when you're doing an appendectomy?

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:49.470
- Oh, looks like some of
these ribs won't budge.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.450
Time for the bone saw.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.460
- Ribs? The appendix is not that high up.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.120
You're not going to the gallbladder.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.120
You're not going for the liver.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.620
You're going for the appendix.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:00.870
It's lower. The ribs aren't in the way.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.400
They don't need to budge.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.110
And what kind of saw is that?

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:04.980
I just saw that.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:05.813
I just saw that.

00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:06.646
You saw that pun?

00:02:06.646 --> 00:02:08.900
Unintentional, but still delicious.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:09.940
- We're losing him!

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.500
Prep a crash cart, give me 50-CCS...

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.353
Wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:02:13.353 --> 00:02:14.680
(moans)

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.792
- First of all, chest compressions,

00:02:16.792 --> 00:02:19.030
chest compressions, chest compressions.

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.145
If you're losing him and
you do not have a pulse,

00:02:21.145 --> 00:02:23.060
this person is no longer living

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.210
and you need to begin CPR.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.680
You don't start saying
you need CCS of anything.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.240
You start doing chest compressions,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.010
even while you're running.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:32.930
Not like that.

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:34.220
- At first I thought the cause

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.610
of death was kidney failure

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.460
brought about by untreated diabetes,

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.463
but then I noticed the knife.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.560
- Diabetes can absolutely
lead to kidney failure.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.020
So can untreated high blood pressure.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.940
A lot of people will
say, " Well I feel fine.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.080
I don't feel the high blood pressure

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:49.913
so I must be okay."

00:02:49.913 --> 00:02:51.240
It's a silent killer folks.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.240
Hypertension can really
wreak havoc on your kidney

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.370
and your blood vessels and your heart,

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.040
which can lead to a stroke, heart attack,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:01.960
congestive heart failure, so many issues.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:04.743
- Okay, time to test those reflexes.

00:03:05.657 --> 00:03:09.060
- (laughs) As a family medicine actor,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.460
I see pediatric patients meaning children.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.220
Seeing a child for the
first time is you need

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:14.950
to check their developmental milestones.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.828
And I remember one of
the things I was trained

00:03:16.828 --> 00:03:18.617
in my med school training was,

00:03:18.617 --> 00:03:21.320
"Why don't you throw a block on the floor

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:22.870
and tell the child to retrieve it

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.920
and see if they're capable
of following instructions."

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.710
And I always felt uncomfortable
taking that advice

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.040
because I'm like, " What
am I gonna play fetch

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:30.980
with a child?"

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.510
Like, " Go get it!"

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.190
- Hi I'm Dr. Web MD.

00:03:34.190 --> 00:03:35.350
So what brings you in here today?

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:36.183
- Well you're free.

00:03:36.183 --> 00:03:37.670
- Too embarrassed to see a real doctor?

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.230
- You got me.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.063
What is it doc?

00:03:40.063 --> 00:03:40.960
- You have cancer.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.450
- How could you possibly know that?

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:44.104
- Oh I tell everyone they have cancer.

00:03:44.104 --> 00:03:46.410
- (laughs) I understand why doing this

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.100
in the middle of the night quickly,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:49.960
in the privacy of your
own home is so appealing.

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:51.320
But the reality of the fact is,

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.380
it's almost always gonna be inaccurate.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.213
Look don't get me wrong.

00:03:54.213 --> 00:03:56.710
Web MD does a great job
at explaining a whole host

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:57.730
of range of conditions

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.760
so that you understand
when you do get a diagnosis

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:00.880
what do you actually have.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.650
Or perhaps how to ask better questions

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.890
of your doctor/nurse,
whoever you're gonna go see.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.260
But a lot of times when you
do the symptom tracker thing,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:08.740
it just tells you you have cancer.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.280
- What if I just say my shin hurts?

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.960
- See, told you cancer.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.990
- I really don't think I have cancer.

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.370
- Nobody ever thinks they have cancer.

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:17.440
The symptoms can take
many different forms,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:22.040
such as throbbing in head, runny nose,

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.210
mild cough, stress.

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.750
- Why was stove burn on that list?

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:29.440
Why does stove burn mean cancer?

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.940
- I'm gonna write you a prescription,

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:32.918
for 10 tips on how to
have a healthy summer.

00:04:32.918 --> 00:04:35.441
Don't forget to bring a beach ball.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:38.270
- (laughs) I feel like
there are articles like that

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:40.410
that keeps getting popped
up and recommended to you

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.970
alongside the little
pharma ads here and there.

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.130
So this is quite accurate thus far.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.560
- I really think that you
should use some Zanoplax.

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.080
You just take a small dab,

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.160
rub it on your belly.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.840
- Enough with the Zanoplax!

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.170
- Sorry about that.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:54.410
They're one of my sponsors.

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:55.420
- You know what? I'm leaving.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.550
- And who are you gonna see?

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.760
You don't have health insurance.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.030
- I know everyone wants
to create an AI platform

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.520
where you type in some symptoms

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.230
and it spits out a diagnosis.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.930
Medicine is complicated.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.690
Medicine is subjective.

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:09.700
Symptoms are subjective.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.500
Pain levels are subjective.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.270
The way people understand medical
terminology is subjective.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.560
So you just need a human being there.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.150
I don't see humans ever
not taking care of humans.

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.490
But at the same time,
(robotic beeping)

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.278
there are robots among us.

00:05:25.278 --> 00:05:26.720
(robotic tone)

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:28.540
So speak to your doctor about any symptoms

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.970
that you may be having and have
a good primary care doctor.

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:32.610
- [Narrator] The Anderson
twins have shared a lung

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:33.940
for 25 years.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.370
Separating conjoined twins

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.220
is the most complicated medical procedure

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.250
we will ever perform.

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:40.750
- I have actually heard, I
don't know if this is accurate,

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:42.430
I could be spreading misinformation.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.240
Someone needs to fact check me on this.

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:48.040
Doing a full head transplant
could be the hardest operation.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.370
See if that's the case.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.450
- This procedure will take
between 19 and 23 hours.

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:55.080
If Dr. Alexander will
note the time, we will be-

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:56.952
- All right chums, let's do this!

00:05:56.952 --> 00:05:59.025
Leeroy Jenkins!

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:00.920
- No, no, no!
(laughs)

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.210
- I do understand the reference!

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.830
That's " World of Warcraft."

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:05.663
- [Cameraman] Yes.

00:06:05.663 --> 00:06:07.080
- Oh my God I remember it.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.226
And then they all ran in
and they all ended up dying.

00:06:09.226 --> 00:06:11.726
- [Game Voice] Leeroy Jenkins!

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.180
- God what a throwback.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:15.280
I used to love " WoW."

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:17.270
It ate so much of my time in high school,

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.513
it's kind of ridiculous.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:25.280
- Okay, let me ask you a few questions.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:27.980
Have you ever uttered the
phrase, "Get off my lawn?"

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:28.813
- No.

00:06:28.813 --> 00:06:31.075
- Okay.
- I mean, yeah I've said.

00:06:31.075 --> 00:06:33.090
But I didn't mean like old man,

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:34.500
like " Get off my lawn!"

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.570
I meant more like, "This is my
property so get off my lawn."

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.110
- I'm not gonna sugarcoat this for you.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:42.060
You have Early Onset Grumpiness.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:43.856
(ominous music)

00:06:43.856 --> 00:06:46.710
- What?
- Early Onset Grumpiness, EOG.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.070
You're gonna begin to enjoy fewer

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:49.720
and fewer things in your life.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:51.920
You'll be saying things like,
" Who are these people?"

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:52.753
- Who are those people?

00:06:52.753 --> 00:06:54.050
That's the thing, I mean
it's a real question.

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:55.310
- The only reason you leave the house

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.720
is to see classic old movies

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:58.920
and even then you'll say,
" It had some good parts

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.410
but all in all it was fairly..."

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:01.243
- Uneven.

00:07:02.993 --> 00:07:03.826
(sighs)

00:07:03.826 --> 00:07:05.440
- You're very young to have EOG.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.810
Your life is gonna change forever.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.570
- What would be the treatment
for Early Onset Grumpiness?

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:11.270
Joy, fun, therapy.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.980
Maybe I'm doing a little stretch here.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.760
There is something called
dysthymia in medicine.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.390
Where you just in general for
an extended period of time,

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.770
have a low mood, like a depressed mood

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:22.510
but you don't have major
depressive disorder,

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:23.610
where you're just kinda down.

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:27.280
Maybe dysthymias can be
nicknamed Early Onset Grumpiness.

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:29.250
Just putting it out there.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:31.160
This looks like it's like the 80s.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.280
I'm getting an 80s vibe.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.093
Ooh, why would you hit the
patient's head like that?

00:07:36.950 --> 00:07:38.633
- Still something missing man.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:41.423
- Hmm, hmm.

00:07:44.900 --> 00:07:45.733
- [Both] Patient!

00:07:45.733 --> 00:07:47.120
- Are they doing a C-section?

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.440
If they are, why are they not scrubbed in?

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.790
Why are they putting
on non-sterile gloves?

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:53.180
I have so many questions.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:54.950
I don't know what all this technology is.

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:57.830
This is stuff from the 80s,
I wasn't around back then.

00:07:57.830 --> 00:08:01.007
I was only born pretty much
in the last month of the 80's.

00:08:01.007 --> 00:08:02.670
- Can I put the tube in the baby's head?

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.036
- Only if I can do the episiotomy.

00:08:04.036 --> 00:08:06.750
- Okay.
- Very good, legs up.

00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:09.034
You can come in, come on in all of you.

00:08:09.034 --> 00:08:11.320
That's it, jolly good, come on.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:13.210
- We do not do that.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:14.270
- I'm the husband.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.810
- I'm sorry, only people
involved are allowed in here.

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:19.170
- We do not just invite a peanut gallery

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:20.470
to watch a delivery.

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.590
Actually nurses probably
do the best job at this,

00:08:22.590 --> 00:08:24.630
at managing who comes
in and out of the room.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:25.690
When I was a medical student,

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:27.780
we actually trained to
first introduce ourselves

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:28.613
to the patient,

00:08:28.613 --> 00:08:29.930
then once the patient
was comfortable with us,

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:31.810
we then introduced ourselves
to the nursing staff

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.700
to make sure that the
nurse can help teach us,

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:35.930
guide us, because they know so much more

00:08:35.930 --> 00:08:37.600
than we do at that given moment.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.520
And probably throughout
their careers as well.

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:42.026
- That's the machine that goes bing.

00:08:42.026 --> 00:08:43.279
(machine binging)

00:08:43.279 --> 00:08:46.000
You see that means your
baby is still alive.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.440
- Oh the vulva is dilating doctor.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:48.410
- Oh yes there is the head.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.744
It has four centimeters,
five, six centimeters.

00:08:51.744 --> 00:08:52.577
- Lights!

00:08:52.577 --> 00:08:55.040
- You can't judge the
cervical dilation visually,

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:57.040
you need to be hands-on with this exam

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.670
and actually do a digital exam.

00:08:58.670 --> 00:08:59.950
From there you can get an estimate

00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:01.700
of what exactly the dilation is.

00:09:01.700 --> 00:09:04.840
If the baby's head is already
visible, you're fully dilated.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.320
You don't need four
centimeters measurements.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:08.764
Like it doesn't make sense.

00:09:08.764 --> 00:09:11.611
- And frightening!
(baby crying)

00:09:11.611 --> 00:09:14.810
There, bring the rough towels!

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.790
- We do not have butcher knives,

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:17.710
so much wrong with this.
- There is the mother.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.720
That's enough. Right sedate her.

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:23.420
- Hi, I'm here for my annual physical.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:24.660
I haven't been in years

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.820
because I didn't have health insurance.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:28.910
- Great. Fill out this stack of paperwork

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.440
that you know none of the
information for and wait an hour

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:33.090
after your scheduled
appointment to be seen.

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.330
- The reason this happens

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:36.110
is because medicine is unpredictable.

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:37.420
We don't know what's gonna happen

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:38.940
when a patient walks into our room.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:40.530
Sometimes a patient
says that their problem

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.350
is in knee and then we
start questioning them

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:43.800
and we find out they're
having a heart attack.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.060
That literally happened to me.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.500
I'm linking that story down below.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:48.530
It started with foot pain,
became a heart attack,

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:49.930
calling 911 and all.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:51.320
- I don't want to tear this paper

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.690
but I don't know why I give a
sh because it's just a paper.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.330
- That paper is the bane of my existence.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:00.370
Not only is it incredibly noisy,

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.679
anytime you do any kind of exam.

00:10:02.679 --> 00:10:04.440
(imitating paper crinkling)

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:05.370
It's everywhere.

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:08.240
But then also like as a
patient, like rotates and flips

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:11.110
and when I'm doing like
a leg exam or back exam,

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:14.300
or do some kind of OMT, it
wraps around the patient

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.160
and ends up trapping them and ripping.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:16.993
Someone needs...

00:10:16.993 --> 00:10:18.550
Elon Musk!

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:19.890
We need you!

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.800
Solve the paper problem.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:25.400
Click here for my most favorite
medical meme review ever.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:26.870
Like I really like this one.

00:10:26.870 --> 00:10:28.170
I think you're gonna love it too.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:29.690
So click for that bad boy.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.681
And a little notice, if you get
this video to 100,000 likes,

00:10:32.681 --> 00:10:35.700
I might make one of my
own medical sketches.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:37.250
And I was an A-list actor

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:39.520
back in my seventh grade drama club days.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:40.360
Just saying.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:42.410
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.393
Memes, click it.

00:10:44.393 --> 00:10:47.060
(upbeat music)

